{{ ansible_managed | comment }}
# Managed by live/ansible/playbooks/1panel/manage-websites.yaml
# Rewritten in full on every run - edits made in the 1Panel dashboard are lost.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE WIDENING THE ALLOW BLOCK. The service behind this proxy
# mounts a catalogue endpoint that takes no credential, so this vhost is not one
# control among several - it is the only thing standing between that endpoint
# and every host on the LAN that can reach this box on 443 with the right SNI.
#
# The allow-list is what makes the name safe, NOT the DNS record. nginx picks a
# site by SNI, so an alias removed from Unbound is removed as a convenience and
# not as a door: `curl --resolve` and a hosts entry both still arrive here.
#
# Its one legitimate client is the tunnel connector VM. Nothing else on the LAN
# has a reason to reach this name, and the public path does not come through
# the LAN at all - it arrives from the connector, which is what the allow below
# names.

# SERVER SCOPE, NOT INSIDE THE location BELOW - AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
# 1Panel includes this file in the site's `server` block, which is how every
# template in this directory can open with a bare `location`. So an allow/deny
# pair at this level is inherited by EVERY location in the vhost, including the
# ones 1Panel generates and this file never sees. Inside `location ^~ /` it
# would only cover the requests that block wins - and proxy-prometheus.conf.j2
# next door records what decides that: an `=` or a longer `^~` is resolved
# first. A deny that a future generated location silently steps around is the
# kind of control that reads correct and is not.
#
# Nothing here needs an exemption. The certificate is renewed on OPNsense and
# pushed into this panel rather than answered for over HTTP, so there is no ACME
# challenge path to keep open - see acme-cert-updater.yaml.
#
# THE ADDRESS IS MEASURED, NOT DERIVED, AND NOTHING RE-MEASURES IT.
# The connector sits on the WAN bridge and this origin on the VLAN, so the
# request crosses OPNsense before nginx sees it, and whether $remote_addr
# survives that crossing was settled by observation rather than by reasoning
# about NAT: two probes issued from the connector in August 2026 were logged
# here with the connector's own address. No real_ip_header, set_real_ip_from or
# real_ip_recursive exists anywhere on this host, so $remote_addr is the TCP
# peer and no client-supplied header can forge it.
#
# THE TELL, because the failure is a confusing one. If that stops being true - a
# manual outbound NAT on the VLAN interface, or the connector moved onto vmbr1 -
# this pair starts denying the only client that should get through, and it
# surfaces as a 403 AFTER a successful sign-in at the edge. That reads like an
# access-control misconfiguration and it is not. Re-take the measurement; do not
# go looking in the edge configuration. ADR-0177 §D.
#
# The value is read at run time from the connector's own cloud-init payload,
# never transcribed - see manage-websites.yaml. It is stored there as a /24 and
# arrives here already stripped; an unstripped prefix would allow the whole home
# LAN and would look correct in the diff.
allow {{ dalang_connector_address }};
deny all;

location ^~ / {
    proxy_pass {{ item.upstream }};

    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header REMOTE-HOST $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Port $server_port;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;

    # Nothing here uploads and nothing here streams, so the stock body cap and
    # the stock buffering both stand. The timeouts are raised only enough to
    # outlast a cold start of the upstream, which is a compose stack on this
    # box rather than a remote guest.
    proxy_connect_timeout 30s;
    proxy_read_timeout 120s;
    proxy_send_timeout 120s;
}
